{
  "validity": "fail",
  "quality": "weak",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "access",
      "location": "obligations[0].answer_attributes / cards[0]['Answer-computation attributes'] = teams.team_name",
      "problem": "The task requires reporting each matching workspace's name (teams.team_name), but the documented API surface contains no operation that looks up or returns a workspace/team name from a team_id or any other route. The runtime_qualifications explicitly state: 'No stored workspace-name lookup, profile writes, purpose writes, named roles or hidden-table operations.' None of the listed endpoints (conversations.*, users.*, chat.*, reactions.*, search.*) expose team_name in their documented responses — only channel-level fields (channel_name, topic_text, purpose_text) and user-level fields are described. This means the requested final output ('give me those workspace names') cannot actually be produced by the actor through any supported API call, even though the actor can correctly discover team_ids and admin/owner roles via user_teams-derived membership flags on users.list/users.info. This is a genuine capability gap, not a solvable-but-hard task: it parallels the excluded categories in the review guidance (e.g., last login, edit history) that are stored but not retrievable through these APIs.",
      "repair": "Either (a) change the answer_attributes/obligation to request only teams.team_id (the field actually derivable through discoverable, documented routes), and adjust the prompt to ask for workspace IDs rather than names, or (b) if workspace-name retrieval is intended to be supported, add/document a real endpoint (e.g., a conversations.info-style team lookup) that returns team_name, and update the runtime_qualifications to remove the 'No stored workspace-name lookup' restriction so it is consistent with the requested answer field."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "All the referent/route/binding logic examined in prior review rounds remains sound (route WORKSPACE→WORKSPACE_MEMBERSHIP→USER is faithfully modeled, matches are grounded in real user_teams rows for 'Robert Chen', negatives correctly cover role-mismatch, person-mismatch, and absence, and the actor now holds genuine workspace membership in T_ADVISORY/T_RESEARCHLAB for authorized discovery). However, the case fails on a fundamental access/capability ground: the task's requested output includes each matching workspace's name (teams.team_name), yet the supplied runtime_qualifications explicitly declare 'No stored workspace-name lookup' as an unsupported capability in this replica, and no documented API operation (conversations.*, users.*, chat.*, reactions.*, search.*) returns team_name in its response. This is exactly the class of defect the review criteria flag: a requested answer field that is stored but not retrievable through the available APIs constitutes an access violation, converting an otherwise well-formed ordinary route case into an unanswerable task. Because the primary deliverable (workspace names) cannot be produced by the actor via any supported mechanism, the case is invalid as constructed, despite its otherwise clean route, bindings, and negative design."
}
